#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105525 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105738 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02d46a44-0f02-5491-8e71-2eb22ad50ff9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Measure of an Angle Using the Isosceles Triangle Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "df6c46c7-a65c-578c-a9b3-e75de73c527a" :question/math-academy-id "e-2718" :question/problem "What is the measure of $\\angle{A}$ in the triangle $\\triangle{ABC}$ below?\n\n![](images/91/91d1c211d33bb42a474242a6895330a3a2b77b50450d8ba7a1b533cb0a45f3b9.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since two sides are congruent, $\\overline{BC} \\cong \\overline{AB}$, we conclude that $\\triangle ABC$ is isosceles. Consequently, the base angles must have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm\\angle C = m\\angle A = x -10^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, since the internal angles of any triangle sum to $180^\\circ$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A + m∠B + m∠C &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n(x - 10^{∘}) + (x + 20^{∘}) + (x - 10^{∘}) &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n3x &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 60^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=60^\\circ$ into the equation for the measure of $\\angle A$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A &= x - 10^{∘} \\\\\n&= 60^{∘} - 10^{∘} \\\\\n&= 50^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\nm\\angle A = 50^\\circ\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105526 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105739 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0690d62b-432c-5d1e-96f2-b43393f6efa9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Measure of an Angle in an Isosceles Triangle Given an Exterior Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "c7ae19b8-aee0-5a64-b7c8-61f3ce75272c" :question/math-academy-id "e-2721" :question/problem "What is the measure of $\\angle{H}$ in the triangle $\\triangle{FGH}$ below?\n\n![](images/b1/b13dd7712b6ed41bbd6ba9db85b489f71fb498c711d5dd67d4dd91a0845b5dcd.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, from the diagram, notice that $\\angle{HFG}$ and $\\angle{EFH}$ are supplementary. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠HFG + m∠EFH &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠HFG + 105^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠HFG &= 75^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\triangle{FGH}$ is an isosceles triangle where $\\overline{GH} \\cong \\overline{FH}$, the base angles must be equal:\n\n$$\nm\\angle{G} = m\\angle{HFG}=75^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, since the interior angles of any triangle sum to $180^\\circ$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠G + m∠HFG + m∠H &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n75^{∘} + 75^{∘} + m∠H &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n150^{∘} + m∠H &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠H &= 30^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105527 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0044ba61-80c8-57cf-80dc-8a17cf980a84" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Measure of an Angle Given Multiple Triangles"}] :question/id #uuid "e44e7e25-f65b-5f40-9aef-cbe55bfd1b53" :question/math-academy-id "e-2726" :question/problem "In the figure below, $m\\angle{CAD}=100^\\circ$. What is the measure of $\\angle{D}$?\n\n![](images/59/59bb54f508b8045eeffb7cdbf547b6be0f074009b3288fb13e115159909782a8.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $\\triangle{ABC}$ is an equilateral triangle. Consequently, all of the interior angles must be equal to $60^\\circ$:\n\n$$\nm\\angle{BAC}=m\\angle{ABC}=m\\angle{C}=60^\\circ\n$$\n\nAlso, since $\\triangle{ADB}$ is an isosceles triangle, the base angles must have equal measure:\n\n$$\nm\\angle{BAD}=m\\angle{ABD}\n$$\n\n![](images/a9/a997837f549d59b2a554bf6bc60d9de3ebf6b4b97188ac3d0f07342fb4af6507.png)\n\nNow, since we're told that\n\n$$\nm\\angle{CAD}=100^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠BAD &= m∠CAD - m∠BAC \\\\\n&= 100^{∘} - 60^{∘} \\\\\n&= 40^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in $\\triangle{BAD}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠BAD + m∠D + m∠ABD &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2m∠BAD + m∠D &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2 \\cdot 40^{∘} + m∠D &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n80^{∘} + m∠D &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠D &= 100^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105584 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c9c2b7ee-d32b-550b-b110-81d04d750c98" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable Given Unknown Lengths in an Isosceles Triangle"}] :question/id #uuid "d9e953e1-5af0-55eb-a861-af6f4ed9b720" :question/math-academy-id "e-4269" :question/problem "![](images/84/842cd509d5d998858936dda5ae07cc17c69cfd83ea86881ba542d8d34bff7cc6.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of $\\overline{AB}$ in the isosceles triangle $ABC$ above?" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\triangle ABC$ is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overline{AB}$, we have that $\\overline{BC} \\cong \\overline{AC}$. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBC &= AC \\\\\n4x - 3 &= 2x + 1 \\\\\n2x &= 4 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into the expression for the measure of $\\overline{AB}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= x + 4 \\\\\n&= (2) + 4 \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AB=6$."}]}
